    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Transmission</span> is the process of transferring energy, motion or power from one point (the source) to another (where it is used). Transmission may be mechanical or electrical.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Mechanical Transmission Systems</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Description</th><th>Example use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gears</td><td>Toothed wheels that mesh together to transmit rotary motion and change speed, torque or direction</td><td>Car gearbox, clock mechanisms</td></tr><tr><td>Pulleys</td><td>Wheels with a groove carrying a belt or rope, used to transmit rotary motion over a distance or to lift loads</td><td>Engine fan belt, block and tackle</td></tr><tr><td>Levers</td><td>Rigid bars that turn about a fixed point (fulcrum) to transmit and multiply force</td><td>Crowbar, wheelbarrow, scissors</td></tr><tr><td>Belt drives</td><td>A belt connects two pulleys of different sizes to transmit rotary motion, often changing speed</td><td>Sewing machines, generators</td></tr><tr><td>Chain drives</td><td>A chain engages sprockets to transmit motion positively without slipping</td><td>Bicycles, motorcycles</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Electrical Transmission Systems</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Electrical power is transmitted from generating stations to consumers through <strong>power lines</strong> at very high voltage to reduce energy loss, then stepped down for use by <strong>transformers</strong>.</p><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Step-up transformer</td><td>Increases voltage (and reduces current) for transmission at the power station</td></tr><tr><td>Transmission lines/cables</td><td>Carry high-voltage electricity over long distances</td></tr><tr><td>Step-down transformer</td><td>Reduces voltage to safe levels near consumers</td></tr><tr><td>Distribution network</td><td>Delivers electricity at usable voltage to homes and industries</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Importance of Efficient Transmission</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Reduces power (I²R) losses as heat in the cables — high voltage means lower current for the same power, so less heat loss.</li><li>Ensures more energy reaches the consumer.</li><li>Reduces the cost of generation per unit delivered.</li><li>Prevents overheating and damage to cables and equipment.</li><li>Improves the reliability and safety of the power supply.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Power loss in a cable is I²R; transmitting at high voltage (low current) for the same power greatly reduces this loss.</div>",
