    "topic": "Uses of Machines",
    "topicCode": "SS3-PHY-03",
    "module": "Energy and Machines",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">machine</span> is any device that makes work easier by changing the size, direction or speed of an applied force.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Key Definitions</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Load</td><td>The resistance or weight to be overcome</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Effort</td><td>The force applied to the machine</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Mechanical Advantage (MA)</td><td>Ratio of load to effort</td><td>MA = Load ÷ Effort</td></tr><tr><td>Velocity Ratio (VR)</td><td>Ratio of distance moved by effort to distance moved by load</td><td>VR = distance moved by effort ÷ distance moved by load</td></tr><tr><td>Efficiency</td><td>How much of the input work is turned into useful output work</td><td>Efficiency = (MA ÷ VR) × 100%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Simple Machines</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Machine</th><th>Example</th><th>Everyday use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lever</td><td>Crowbar, see-saw, wheelbarrow</td><td>Lifting or moving heavy loads with less effort</td></tr><tr><td>Pulley</td><td>Single and combined pulley systems</td><td>Lifting loads such as a bucket from a well, cranes</td></tr><tr><td>Wheel and axle</td><td>Doorknob, steering wheel, windlass</td><td>Turning a small force into a larger turning effect</td></tr><tr><td>Inclined plane</td><td>Ramp, staircase</td><td>Raising loads gradually with less force</td></tr><tr><td>Screw</td><td>Bolt, jack, drill bit</td><td>Fastening and lifting with mechanical advantage</td></tr><tr><td>Wedge</td><td>Axe, knife, chisel</td><td>Splitting or cutting with concentrated force</td></tr><tr><td>Hydraulic press</td><td>Car jack, hydraulic brakes</td><td>Multiplying force using Pascal's principle in a fluid</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Applications of Machines in Everyday Life</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Cranes use pulley and lever systems to lift heavy building materials.</li><li>Hydraulic presses use Pascal's principle (pressure is transmitted equally in a fluid) to lift cars in workshops.</li><li>The wheel and axle in a steering wheel lets a small force turn the front wheels of a car.</li><li>Levers in scissors and pliers multiply the cutting or gripping force.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Efficiency is always less than 100% because of friction; MA is usually less than VR in a real machine.</div>",
