    "module": "Energy and Machines",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Repair</span> means fixing a machine that has broken down or malfunctioned, while <span class=\"learn-keyword\">maintenance</span> means the routine care given to a machine to keep it working well and prevent breakdowns.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Common Faults in Machines</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Wear and tear of moving parts due to friction.</li><li>Lack of lubrication causing overheating and excessive wear.</li><li>Corrosion or rusting of metal parts, especially in humid conditions.</li><li>Loose or broken components (bolts, belts, gears).</li><li>Electrical faults such as short circuits, worn insulation or blown fuses.</li><li>Blockage or accumulation of dirt and dust in moving parts.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Maintenance</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Preventive (routine) maintenance</td><td>Regular scheduled checks and servicing carried out before a fault occurs, to prevent breakdown</td><td>Oiling a machine regularly, changing engine oil every few months, cleaning filters</td></tr><tr><td>Corrective (breakdown) maintenance</td><td>Repair carried out after a machine has already broken down or failed</td><td>Replacing a burnt-out motor, fixing a punctured tyre</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Importance of Lubrication, Cleaning and Routine Checks</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li><strong>Lubrication</strong> reduces friction between moving parts, reducing wear and heat generation, and improving efficiency.</li><li><strong>Cleaning</strong> removes dust and debris that could cause blockage, corrosion or increased friction.</li><li><strong>Routine checks</strong> detect faults early, before they cause major breakdowns, saving cost and downtime.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Benefits of Good Maintenance</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Extends the working life of machines.</li><li>Reduces the cost of major repairs and replacement.</li><li>Improves safety for operators.</li><li>Keeps machines working at maximum efficiency.</li><li>Reduces unplanned downtime in industry.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Preventive maintenance is carried out BEFORE a fault occurs; corrective maintenance is carried out AFTER a fault has occurred.</div>",
