    "topic": "Dams and Energy Production",
    "topicCode": "SS3-PHY-05",
    "module": "Energy and Machines",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">dam</span> is a large barrier built across a river to hold back water, creating a reservoir. Dams are used for irrigation, flood control, water supply and generating <span class=\"learn-keyword\">hydroelectric power</span>.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Dams</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Main purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hydroelectric dam</td><td>Generates electricity by using the force of falling or flowing water to turn turbines</td></tr><tr><td>Irrigation dam</td><td>Stores water for supplying farmland during the dry season</td></tr><tr><td>Multipurpose dam</td><td>Combines uses — electricity generation, irrigation, flood control and water supply</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. How Hydroelectric Power is Generated</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Water is stored behind the dam wall in a reservoir at a height.</li><li>The stored water is released through a controlled channel (penstock) and falls under gravity, gaining kinetic energy.</li><li>The moving water turns the blades of a turbine.</li><li>The turbine is connected to a generator, which converts the mechanical (rotational) energy into electrical energy.</li><li>The electricity produced is transmitted through power lines to consumers.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Examples of Dams in Nigeria</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Dam</th><th>River / State</th><th>Main use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kainji Dam</td><td>River Niger, Niger State</td><td>Hydroelectric power generation</td></tr><tr><td>Shiroro Dam</td><td>River Kaduna, Niger State</td><td>Hydroelectric power generation</td></tr><tr><td>Jebba Dam</td><td>River Niger, Kwara State</td><td>Hydroelectric power generation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Advantages and Disadvantages of Dams</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Renewable and clean source of electricity</td><td>High cost of construction</td></tr><tr><td>Provides water for irrigation and domestic use</td><td>Displacement of communities from flooded areas</td></tr><tr><td>Controls flooding downstream</td><td>Disruption of aquatic ecosystems and fish migration</td></tr><tr><td>Supports fishing and recreation on the reservoir</td><td>Silting of the reservoir reduces its capacity over time</td></tr><tr><td>Long-lasting once built, with low running cost</td><td>Risk of dam failure causing catastrophic flooding</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> In hydroelectric generation, the energy conversion sequence is: potential energy of stored water → kinetic energy of falling water → mechanical energy of the turbine → electrical energy from the generator.</div>",
