    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">rocket</span> is a vehicle that propels itself by expelling exhaust gas at high speed, working by <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Newton's third law of motion</span>: for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Principle of Rocket Propulsion</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">A rocket burns fuel to produce hot gases, which are forced out of the rear at high speed (the action). By Newton's third law, this pushes the rocket forward in the opposite direction with equal force (the reaction). Rockets can work in the vacuum of space because they carry their own oxidiser and do not need air to push against.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Rockets</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Solid-fuel rockets</td><td>Use solid propellant mixed with an oxidiser; simple, reliable, but cannot be easily stopped once ignited</td></tr><tr><td>Liquid-fuel rockets</td><td>Use liquid fuel and liquid oxidiser stored in separate tanks and pumped into a combustion chamber; can be throttled, stopped and restarted, giving more control</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Types of Satellites</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Communication satellites</td><td>Relay television, telephone and internet signals over long distances</td></tr><tr><td>Navigation satellites</td><td>Provide positioning data, e.g. the Global Positioning System (GPS)</td></tr><tr><td>Weather satellites</td><td>Monitor cloud patterns, storms and climate for forecasting</td></tr><tr><td>Military/reconnaissance satellites</td><td>Used for surveillance and national security</td></tr><tr><td>Earth observation/remote-sensing satellites</td><td>Monitor land use, agriculture, disaster and environmental change</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Importance of Satellites in Modern Technology</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Enable global telecommunications, television broadcasting and internet access.</li><li>Provide accurate weather forecasting and disaster early-warning systems.</li><li>Support navigation for aviation, shipping and personal devices (GPS).</li><li>Allow monitoring of the environment, agriculture and natural resources.</li><li>Support scientific research and national security.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Rocket propulsion is explained by Newton's third law, not Newton's first or second law — remember 'action and reaction are equal and opposite'.</div>",
